.TH "SERIALPORT.WRITABLE" "3JS" "May 19, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
SerialPort.writable \- SerialPort: writable property
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]Secure context:\f[R] This feature is available only in secure
contexts (HTTPS).
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] This feature is available in Dedicated Web Workers.
.PP
The \f[B]\f[CB]writable\f[B]\f[R] read\-only property of the
\f[CR]SerialPort\f[R] interface returns a \f[CR]WritableStream\f[R] for
sending data to the device connected to the port.
Chunks written to this stream must be instances of
\f[CR]ArrayBuffer\f[R], \f[CR]TypedArray\f[R], or \f[CR]DataView\f[R].
This property is non\-null as long as the port is open and has not
encountered a fatal error.
.SH VALUE
A \f[CR]WritableStream\f[R].
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Writing a string to a port
The following example shows how to write a string to a port.
A \f[CR]TextEncoder\f[R] converts the string to a \f[CR]Uint8Array\f[R]
before transmission.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] encoder = \f[B]new\f[R] TextEncoder();
\f[B]const\f[R] writer = port.writable.getWriter();
\f[B]await\f[R] writer.write(encoder.encode(\(dqPING\(dq));
writer.releaseLock();
.EE
